                        "Information": [
                            {
                                "ReferenceNumber": 1,
                                "Value": {
                                    "StringWithMarkup": [
                                        {
                                            "String": "Ginseng is the famous oriental medicinal herb Panax sps used as a drug since ancient times. The \nactive ingredients in ginseng are glycosylated triterpene saponins called ginsenosides [PMID: 12920566]. \nMore than 30 kinds of ginseosides are known and based on the aglycone skeleton they are classified into \ntwo groups; the dammarenediol-type and oleanane-type [PMID: 16962103]. The Ginsenosides differ from \none another only by the type of sugar moieties, their number, their sites of attachment and the number of \nhydroxyl groups [Yue05] but the biological activities of the ginsenosides differ extensively. \n\nThe pharmacological activities of various ginsenosides include cardio-protective, immuno-modulatory, \nanti-fatigue, antitumour and hepato-protective effects [Yue05]. The roots of Panax ginseng contain \nthe triterpene saponins and undergoe specfic developmental changes to function as an organ that \nsynthesizes the ginsenosides [LUO05]. The various types of ginsenosides accumulate with age \n[PMID: 15356323]. \n\nThe chemical synthesis of ginsenosides has not been been feasible and little is known about the genes \nand biochemical pathways. The ginsenoside backbones are synthesized from the \nPOLYISOPRENSYN-PWY by cyclization of  EPOXYSQUALENE \n[PMID: 15356323]. The cyclization leads to a branch point of sterol and triterpene pathways. \nBased on the aglycone backbone they are classified into 3 types: protopanaxdiol-type, \nprotopanaxtriol-type, and oleanane-type saponins [PMID: 12920566]. The triterpene backbone \nundergoes various modifications, such as oxidation, substitution, and glycosylation to give the \ncharacteristic ginsenosides.",
